From 4568750149d74e86e8da808035ae3e5aa0665d05 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vasilii Zolotukhin <vasilii.zolotukhin@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 20:54:11 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 8/9] Mac: Cmd+Shift+T and the focus after closing follow the
 unified strip

Chrome keeps recently closed tabs per profile, so Cmd+Shift+T (and File
> Reopen Closed Tab) reopened a tab of the focused window's profile:
closing the only tab of one profile moved the focus to another profile's
window, and Cmd+Shift+T then reopened that profile's tab, or a page in
the wrong profile.

The unified strip now records which profile each closed tab belonged to
and where it was. Cmd+Shift+T (caught by the key monitor) and the menu
item (retargeted) pick the newest entry of all profiles: Chrome's own
entries for profiles with a window, the recorded closes for the others.
It reopens through that profile's tab restore service, loading the
profile and opening a window for it if its last window had closed, and
the tab goes back to its place in the strip. With nothing recorded,
Chrome's command runs as before.

When the active tab is closed, its neighbor in the unified strip (the
next tab, else the previous one) becomes active, also when it belongs
to another profile, instead of a tab Chrome or AppKit picks.
